  font-weight: 700 !important;
}


/* ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   5. DISPLAY SERIF UNIFICATION — Fraunces → Instrument Serif
   
   Fraunces appears in 3 places where the declared display face
   is Instrument Serif. They're both variable-weight oldstyle
   serifs but they're different typefaces — mixing them at the
   same role creates inconsistency at display sizes.
   
   Instrument Serif is the declared display face. Fraunces
   fallbacks are fine (they share Georgia as a final fallback)
   but the primary name should be consistent.
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

.db-kpi-val,
.db-greeting-title {
  font-family: 'Instrument Serif', 'Fraunces', Georgia, serif !important;
}


/* ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   6. RESEARCH HUB + PUBLICATIONS — elevated type hierarchy
   
